{"purpose":"Assess EKA product identity, strategic value, overengineering risk, competitive position, and evidence required to decide its future direction.","content":"## Executive verdict\nEKA is not useless and is not inherently a bad idea. It is over-scoped as a product and technically credible as a narrow engineering-knowledge control plane. EKA should not compete directly with coding agents, IDEs, autonomous execution systems, or mature code-search products.\n\nRecommended identity:\n\n\u003e Agent-neutral engineering knowledge control plane.\n\nCoding agents execute. EKA preserves engineering truth.\n\n## Problem severity\nThe problem is real but unevenly distributed. Solo developers and small teams often gain little from formal knowledge governance. Large, regulated, multi-repository, architecture-heavy, and AI-agent-heavy organizations face higher costs from lost decisions, repeated clarification, unsafe agent changes, weak handoffs, and missing traceability.\n\nEKA should sell outcomes, not model concepts. Valuable outcomes include fewer rejected or reworked PRs, faster onboarding, fewer architecture violations, safer agent changes, lower repeated clarification, better cross-agent handoff, and cheaper auditability.\n\n## Current strengths\n- Explicit typed engineering knowledge model.\n- Discovery → Architecture → Planning → Execution → Operations authority chain.\n- Immutable revisions, provenance, identity, and history.\n- Typed relationships and deterministic context resolution.\n- Validation, integrity checks, state transitions, and traceability gates.\n- Agent-neutral CLI/MCP access.\n- Clear separation between source code, authoring, runtime knowledge, and transport.\n\n## Current weaknesses and risks\n- No demonstrated external paying users or sustained external usage.\n- Adoption requires learning concepts and maintaining structured knowledge.\n- Product surface combines knowledge standard, runtime, workflow governance, planning, execution, MCP, and planned code graph.\n- Code-context is currently incomplete; existing code-context execution records do not prove source implementation.\n- Stale or incorrect knowledge can create false confidence.\n- EKA does not yet enforce model token budgets or provide complete session continuity.\n- Technology components such as CLI, MCP, SQLite, JSON, graph traversal, and indexing are not a moat by themselves.\n- If users mainly request IDE, code search, or autonomous coding, EKA is solving the wrong product problem.\n\n## Competitive position\nCursor, Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot coding agent, Devin, Sourcegraph Cody, Greptile, Continue, and Aider are more mature in their respective coding-agent, IDE, execution, code-search, or repository-map categories. EKA is not broadly more mature than these products.\n\nEKA can differentiate narrowly through durable, typed, immutable, deterministic engineering knowledge: why a decision exists, which constraint binds work, what superseded what, what scope is approved, how state changed, and what evidence supports completion. This is a potential workflow/data moat, not a current technology moat. It becomes defensible only after trusted, repeated organizational usage.\n\n## Code-context and token evidence\nExisting EKA research records claim:\n- five full files from grep: approximately 9,400 tokens;\n- symbol + one-hop code context: approximately 72% code-token reduction;\n- estimated after-context: approximately 2,632 tokens;\n- reference reduction: approximately 78%;\n- noise reduction: approximately 81%;\n- precision@1: 0.33 → 0.58;\n- precision@3: 0.58 → 0.83;\n- task success: 5/12 → 8/12;\n- indexing cold cost: 1.8–2.4 seconds per 1,000 files;\n- incremental cost: 45–90ms for 10 files;\n- determinism: 98.7%.\n\nThese are research claims and useful hypotheses, not universal guarantees. Current repository review did not find a complete reproducible benchmark harness, tokenizer specification, raw fixture corpus, or per-task output supporting independent replication. Exact provider-token savings require provider tokenizer measurements.\n\nKnowledge context also consumes tokens. Sample proxy measurements show compact CKO/context outputs around 400–1,582 proxy tokens and engineering context around 819–4,581 proxy tokens. Total request cost is:\n\nT_total = task + knowledge context + code context + tool output + retrieval rounds.\n\nTherefore -72% code-context reduction does not equal -72% total session reduction. Poor retrieval policy can turn EKA into a token tax. Progressive disclosure is preferred: compact knowledge first, code level 0, then level 1/2/3 only when needed.\n\n## Recommended product boundary\nEKA should own:\n- engineering intent;\n- architecture constraints and decisions;\n- approved scope and task lineage;\n- review evidence and state transitions;\n- cross-agent handoff;\n- freshness and conflict signals.\n\nEKA should not try to own all of:\n- IDE experience;\n- model selection;\n- autonomous code execution;\n- general code search;\n- full LSP/type resolution;\n- vector database or RAG as canonical source.\n\nCode intelligence should be consumed from or integrated with tree-sitter, LSP, Sourcegraph, IDE indexes, or native agent search when that is cheaper and more mature. EKA code-context can provide a deterministic bounded layer, but should not become a second Sourcegraph without evidence.\n\nRAG may be investigated later as optional plugin enrichment. It must not become primary deterministic graph source and must never enter CKO, snapshot, or canonical cache. RAG results require provenance and confidence and must not alter deterministic results when unavailable.\n\n## 90-day validation experiment\nUse 3–5 real repositories, two agent types, and 30–50 real engineering tasks. Compare baseline workflow against EKA-assisted workflow.\n\nMeasure:\n- time to first correct implementation;\n- task success rate;\n- rejected or reworked PRs;\n- rework cycles;\n- architecture violations;\n- repeated clarification count;\n- context tokens/bytes;\n- retrieval rounds;\n- cross-agent handoff success;\n- stale or conflicting knowledge incidents;\n- time required to create and maintain knowledge.\n\nContinue and invest only if evidence approaches:\n- at least 15% fewer rejected or reworked PRs;\n- at least 20% fewer repeated architecture clarifications;\n- at least 25% lower relevant-context payload without lower task success;\n- at least 80% successful cross-agent handoffs;\n- developers retrieve EKA context without manual coaching;\n- at least two teams continue after pilot.\n\n## Kill criteria\nNarrow, pivot, or stop if after a serious pilot:\n- no measurable improvement in PR quality, rework, or task success;\n- agents ignore EKA unless manually prompted every task;\n- knowledge maintenance cost exceeds saved engineering time;\n- EKA performs no better than AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md + issue/PR context + native agent search;\n- per-agent integration maintenance dominates product value;\n- teams will not promote decisions into EKA;\n- customers primarily want IDE, code search, or autonomous coding;\n- no team accepts EKA as source of truth for one real architecture or workflow boundary.\n\n## Strategic recommendation\nStop expanding standards and platform surface until the narrow value is proven. Finish the smallest code-context MVP, build a reproducible benchmark, integrate one agent end-to-end, and measure real task outcomes. Delete or defer features that do not improve those outcomes. If the pilot fails, retain EKA as a Git-native architecture/decision validator or internal compliance and traceability library rather than forcing it into a universal agent platform.\n\n## Evidence classification\nMeasured or directly observed: current EKA runtime/CLI architecture, existing context behavior, current code-context stub, workspace validation/integrity results, sample serialized output sizes, and competitor capabilities documented by their official sources.\n\nDerived: approximately 2,632 tokens after 72% reduction; combined request examples; percentage reductions after adding knowledge context.\n\nResearch claims: benchmark metrics recorded in ADR-034 and related EKA findings.\n\nJudgment: product positioning, overengineering assessment, moat analysis, recommended boundary, pilot thresholds, and kill criteria.","investigationSummary":"EKA is technically serious but commercially unproven. Its real problem is engineering context loss: agents and teams can ignore intent, architecture constraints, approved scope, decision history, and handoff evidence while changing code. EKA addresses this with typed Engineering Knowledge, immutable CKOs, relationships, strata authority, deterministic context, validation, and governed lifecycle. Current implementation is stronger as a knowledge/control-plane foundation than as a coding agent. Code-context remains incomplete: cmd/code_context.go is currently a stub, while existing knowledge records describe a proposed deterministic derived code graph. The central strategic question is whether EKA improves real software-development outcomes enough to justify its adoption and maintenance cost.","conclusion":"EKA is a credible technical foundation with a real but segmented problem. It is not yet a validated product. Broad expansion would be overengineering. The rational path is narrow positioning as an agent-neutral engineering knowledge control plane, integration with existing coding agents, reproducible outcome measurement, and willingness to narrow or stop if EKA does not beat simpler combinations such as AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, issue/PR context, Git, and native agent search."}